# File naming

A filename must identify what is in the file on its own, without the directory as context. Grep
output, tracebacks and editor tabs all show the basename alone, and a tree full of `service.py`,
`client.py` and `config.py` is unreadable in all three.

```
GOOD  services/github/github_adapter.py     logger/log_manager.py
BAD   services/github/adapter.py            logger/manager.py
```

- `snake_case`. A hyphenated module cannot be imported.
- Repeat the concept even when the directory already states it. The redundancy costs nothing and
  the basename has to stand alone.
- No catch-all modules: `utils.py`, `helpers.py`, `common.py`, `misc.py`. If nothing more specific
  fits, the contents do not belong together.
- One primary export, named after the file: `log_manager.py` exports `LogManager`,
  `cancel_on_sigterm.py` exports `cancel_on_sigterm`.

## Directory layout

Clients, services, and models keep a subdirectory per resource or domain from the start. A
client is `clients/<resource>/<resource>_client.py` (`clients/github/github_client.py`). A
use case is `*_service.py` / `*_adapter.py` / `*_writer.py` plus `*_errors.py`. A domain
accumulates schemas (`models/github/github_repo_info.py`). Nesting never shortens a
filename. The basename still has to stand alone.

Cross-cutting packages (`config/`, `logger/`, `distribution/`), `lifecycle/`, and
`task_runner/` stay flat: sibling modules, one concept each. Nest one only when a single concept
grows a second module.
